<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "CSuiteFinder — reach the decision-maker",
      description: "Turn a name and a company into a verified work email address or a direct phone number. $#{@seat_usd} a month per seat for sales teams, or a usage-priced API for developers.",
      nav: :none,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:landing)
    ) %>
<header>
  <h1>Reach the decision-maker, not the contact form.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    You know the person you need — a VP of Engineering, a founder, a Head of Procurement.
    CSuiteFinder turns their name and their company into a verified work email address
    and a direct phone number.
  </p>
</header>

<h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">Which one are you?</h2>

<p class="quiet" style="margin-top:-6px;margin-bottom:22px">
  Same data, two products. Pick the door that matches how you work.
</p>

<div class="doors">
  <a class="door" href="/teams">
    <span class="tag">Sales, founders and recruiters</span>
    <h3 class="door-title">I need to reach people</h3>
    <span class="price">$<%= @seat_usd %><span class="per">/month per seat</span></span>
    <p>
      Work in the browser. Search a company, get the room — names, titles, deliverable
      addresses and direct numbers. No API key, no terminal, no meter to watch.
    </p>
    <span class="go">See the seat plan →</span>
  </a>

  <a class="door" href="/developers">
    <span class="tag">Engineers and agents</span>
    <h3 class="door-title">I need an API</h3>
    <span class="price">Usage-priced<span class="per">pay per answer</span></span>
    <p>
      A plain JSON API over HTTPS. No SDK, no seat licence, no sales call. Register with
      one <code>POST</code> and you have a key.
    </p>
    <span class="go">See the API and prices →</span>
  </a>
</div>

<p class="cta-note" style="margin-top:18px">
  Not sure yet? Both start the same way —
  <a href="/account"><%= @trial_usd %> of free credit</a>, no card, no call.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label">What it does, either way</h2>

<div class="does">
  <div class="item"><strong>Find the people at a company</strong><span>A domain in, named people out — with titles, departments and addresses. Filter to executives for the C-suite.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Find an email</strong><span>A name and a company domain become a work email address.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Find a phone number</strong><span>A direct line or mobile for the same person, with the carrier and line type — and we remember whose it is.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Check it's deliverable</strong><span>Confirm the mailbox is real before you send, so bounces don't hurt your sender reputation.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Identify an address</strong><span>Given an email, find out whose it is — name, job title, employer.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Identify a number</strong><span>A phone number you were called from, matched back to the person and their company.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Learn a company's format</strong><span>See how a company builds its addresses, and apply it to anyone else there.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Look up the company</strong><span>Industry, headcount, founding year and location behind any work address.</span></div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">Why the addresses are good</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  Over 95% of lookups resolve, at a fraction of a cent each rather than the per-contact
  dollars a seat-priced database charges — and fast enough to sit inside a workflow.
</p>
<p class="quiet">
  Every address can be verified against the live mailbox before you use it. That check is
  included on both plans, and it is the single best thing you can do for your domain's
  inbox rate: a bounced cold email costs you far more than the lookup did.
</p>

<div class="hero-cta" style="margin-top:26px">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/teams">For sales teams — $<%= @seat_usd %>/mo</a>
  <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers">For developers — the API</a>
</div>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer() %>

</div>
</body>
</html>
